
.TH DIRNAME 1 "October 2026" "dirname" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dirname \- strip the last component from a pathname
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirname
.RB [ \-z ]
.IR NAME ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dirname
outputs the directory portion of each specified pathname,
removing the final component.
.PP
Trailing slashes are removed before processing.
If a pathname contains no slash, the result is
.BR . .
If the pathname consists entirely of slashes, the result is
.BR / .
.PP
Multiple pathnames may be specified, and each result is
printed on a separate line by default.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
End each output with a NUL byte instead of a newline.
This allows safe processing of pathnames containing
newline characters.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Extract the directory portion of a pathname:
.PP
.RS
.B dirname /usr/local/bin/gcc
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B /usr/local/bin
.RE
.PP
Process multiple pathnames:
.PP
.RS
.B dirname /usr/bin/gcc /etc/passwd
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/usr/bin
/etc
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pathname without a slash produces
.BR . :
.PP
.RS
.B dirname file.txt
.RE
.PP
Produce NUL-terminated output:
.PP
.RS
.B dirname -z /usr/local/bin/gcc
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option was specified, no pathname was supplied,
or an output error occurred.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR dirname .
The
.B \-z
option, support for multiple pathnames, and the
.B \-\-help
option are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR basename (1),
.BR realpath (1)

